.TH SCHEDUTIL\-SETUP 8 "2026-11-22" "pitch 1.2.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
schedutil\-setup \- enable, verify and remove the schedutil CPU frequency policy that pitch needs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B schedutil\-setup
.BR status " | " check " | " verify
.br
.B schedutil\-setup install
.RB [ \-\-now ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B schedutil\-setup apply
.br
.B schedutil\-setup uninstall
.RB [ \-\-now ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B schedutil\-setup
puts a machine on the CPU frequency policy that
.BR pitch (1)
works with, and that on its own saves power on loaded machines:
.IP \(bu 2
the cpufreq driver in passive mode
.RB ( intel_pstate=passive
on Intel,
.B amd_pstate=passive
on AMD), so a governor decides the frequency;
.IP \(bu
the
.B schedutil
governor
.RB ( cpufreq.default_governor=schedutil ),
which follows the scheduler's own per-task utilization (and with it the clamps
.B pitch
sets);
.IP \(bu
.BR kernel.sched_util_clamp_min_rt_default=0 .
Without it every runnable realtime task asks schedutil for the top frequency, and on server CPUs whose uncore clock
follows the highest request one periodic realtime task holds the whole package up.
.PP
The first two go on the kernel command line, the third into
.IR /etc/sysctl.d .
Measured at equal completed work, this policy drew 70\(en97 W less than ondemand at 10\(en50 % load on a 4\-socket Xeon
server, and 11\(en21 W less than intel_pstate active/powersave at 25\(en50 % on a Xeon W workstation; it was equal
elsewhere.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Show the driver and its mode, the governors, the realtime clamp, whether the boot arguments are live, the files this
command manages, and every conflict (see
.BR CONFLICTS ).
Read\-only; any user.
.TP
.B check
Only the checks from
.BR status .
Exit status 0 if
.B install
may proceed.
.TP
.B install
Write the boot arguments (a GRUB drop\-in and
.BR update\-grub ,
or
.B grubby
where that is the bootloader), the sysctl file, and the cpufrequtils pin when that package is installed. Record the
running driver mode, governor and realtime clamp in
.I /var/lib/schedutil\-setup/state
for
.BR "uninstall \-\-now" .
Nothing is changed while a conflict is found or the system is unsupported.
.TP
.B verify
Exit status 0 only if every setting is live: boot arguments on the running kernel's command line, driver in passive
mode, every cpufreq policy on schedutil, realtime clamp 0, sysctl file present, cpufrequtils pinned. Run it after
every reboot or kernel update; usable from monitoring.
.TP
.B apply
Switch the running system to the policy without writing anything; a reboot returns to the configured state.
.TP
.B uninstall
Remove the files this command wrote and restore any file it had backed up. With
.BR \-\-now ,
also switch the running system back to the recorded mode, governor and realtime clamp (driver defaults if the
recorded state already was this policy).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-now
With
.BR install :
also switch the running system (the cpufreq driver mode can change at run time, no reboot is needed to start).
With
.BR uninstall :
also switch it back.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Proceed despite conflicts, a kernel without utilization clamping, or the untested amd\-pstate driver.
.TP
.BR \-\-dry\-run ", " \-n
Print every change instead of making it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/default/grub.d/96\-cpufreq\-schedutil.cfg
the boot arguments (Debian and Ubuntu GRUB).
.TP
.I /etc/sysctl.d/60\-schedutil\-rt\-clamp.conf
.BR "kernel.sched_util_clamp_min_rt_default = 0" .
.TP
.I /etc/default/cpufrequtils
.B GOVERNOR="schedutil"
when the cpufrequtils package is installed. Without this file its boot script selects ondemand, which in passive mode
silently replaces schedutil at every boot. An existing file is edited in place (only its GOVERNOR line) and restored
on uninstall.
.TP
.I /var/lib/schedutil\-setup/
.I state
(the pre\-install mode, governor and clamp),
.I backup/
(files restored by uninstall),
.I adopted/
(hand\-written files that already carried the same setting: kept for reference, never restored).
.TP
.I /usr/share/pitch/schedutil/
stock copies of the three files (the GRUB drop\-in with the Intel arguments). They are inert;
.B install
writes the live files from them, and configuration\-management tools can copy them as they are (a hand\-copied file
that already carries the setting is adopted).
.PP
Files this command writes start with a
.B "# Managed by schedutil\-setup"
line; it removes only files carrying it.
.SH CONFLICTS
.B check
and
.B install
look for anything else that sets the governor or the driver mode, and
.B install
refuses while one is found: conflicting
.BR intel_pstate= ,
.B amd_pstate=
or
.B cpufreq.default_governor=
values in
.I /etc/default/grub
or another drop\-in or on the running command line; enabled tuned, auto\-cpufreq or cpupower services; TLP with
.BR CPU_SCALING_GOVERNOR_ON_AC / BAT ;
udev rules, systemd units, sysfs.conf, tmpfiles, cron or rc.local entries that write
.I scaling_governor
or the driver status; intel\-powermode configured with another passive governor. It reports, but does not block on,
power\-profiles\-daemon (in passive mode it cannot set EPP) and thermald (compatible).
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Linux 5.10 or later with
.B CONFIG_CPU_FREQ_GOV_SCHEDUTIL
and
.BR CONFIG_UCLAMP_TASK ,
a cpufreq driver (most virtual machines have none: nothing to do), and Debian/Ubuntu GRUB
.RB ( update\-grub )
or
.BR grubby .
Tested on Ubuntu 24.04 with Intel Xeon (Cascade Lake SP and W) under intel_pstate; the grubby and amd\-pstate paths are
untested.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success
.TP
.B 1
.B verify
or
.B check
found a problem
.TP
.B 2
usage error
.TP
.B 3
unsupported system (nothing changed)
.TP
.B 4
conflicts found (nothing changed; use
.B \-\-force
to override)
.TP
.B 5
needs root
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
schedutil\-setup status
sudo schedutil\-setup install \-\-dry\-run
sudo schedutil\-setup install \-\-now      # live now, and at every boot
sudo reboot
sudo schedutil\-setup verify
pitch \-n \-20 \-\- perl \-e '...'           # now the clamps take effect
sudo schedutil\-setup uninstall \-\-now    # back out
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pitch (1),
.BR sysctl (8),
.BR update\-grub (8),
.BR grubby (8),
.BR cpufreq\-set (1)
